{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.domain`\n\nThe {term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain` implements\n`trust_but_verify::domain::Domain` to define its node-identity type. Its\nerror type is introduced in {cite}`requirements.sdk.errors`:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Domain: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    /// The *identifier* identifies a particular node in the graph.\n    type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.graph.domain_types` says the application domain implements `Domain` and defines its node-identity type as `type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`. The testable content is:\n- A domain can supply a `NodeId` type that has exactly these bounds.\n- The `Domain` trait itself requires `NodeId` to be `Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`. Neither fewer nor more bounds are allowed.\n- Distinct identities are distinguishable and comparable, and they work as the node identity across node, edge and evidence operations.\n\n**Tests reviewed.**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]`\n  - The fixture's `Identity` derives only `Clone, PartialEq, Eq`. It has no `Debug` or `Hash`. The `Domain` impl compiles with it, which rules out the trait demanding extra bounds on `NodeId`.\n  - The generic function `check_bounds<G: Domain>` calls `identity::<G::NodeId>()`, where `identity` requires `Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`. If the trait loosened any of those bounds, this would fail to compile.\n  - Runtime use covers `node_path`, `outgoing_edges` and `load_evidence`/`store_evidence` with that identity. `assert_ne!` on paths checks that distinct ids are distinguished. `outgoing_edges` is checked for duplicate ids and for ids with no successors.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[enum_node_ids_distinguish_requirements_and_source_items_in_one_domain]`\n  - A realistic enum `NodeId` (`Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq`) is used end to end. `assert_ne!(requirement, source)` checks `Eq`. Clones are used to store and compare evidence.\n  - Dependencies are recorded and compared by id equality. `load_evidence` is checked for the stored node and for a different node.\n- Other marked tests use the node identity in the other `Domain` implementations: `src/sdk.rs` for `HabeasDomain`, `src/authoring/sdk.rs` for `ReviewGraph`, and the `Memory` fixture in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs`. They check that paths, content, edges and dependencies work with the node-id type. `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` does the same for the `Application` domain.\n\n**Assessment.** The only normative content is the bound set on `NodeId` and its use as node identity. The compile-time generic bound check and the minimal-bounds opaque fixture catch both loosened and over-tightened bounds. The runtime assertions cover identity comparison and use. The `Error` type is introduced by `requirements.sdk.errors` and is cited context, so I did not reassess it. I found no gap and no FIXME.",
  "references": "8f371c4ec7f0c0885da5414a2e1353bd9a2d1a595b7e6b3d293f799812f87832",
